<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.2.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01264</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.2.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2651c5b1-75ed-41ad-b9a8-910134ec83e3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
    <section class="check-understanding">
      <exercise id="_7b294637-4116-4521-8f36-19acc0b541f1" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Simplify \(\sqrt[4]{81}\).</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.2.2.
     </para>
          <para>The correct answer is 3.
     </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_23470c41-fa61-4971-9d7c-0073637a54b2" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Simplify \(\sqrt[5]{-32}\).</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.2.2.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is –2.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="b1faaa52-5909-4d5e-9eb8-b043c84f2ebf" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Which of the following is equivalent to \(\sqrt[4]{x^3}\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x^\frac34\)</para><para>\(x^\frac43\)</para><para>\(4x^3\)</para><para>\(\frac34x\)</para></problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.2.3.
     </para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(x^\frac34\).
     </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_2fc21da6-2cb8-4955-954d-e1426c564ce9" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Which of the following is equivalent to \(\sqrt[9]{2^4}\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(2^\frac94\)</para><para>\(\frac49(2)\)</para><para>\(\frac{2^4}9\)</para><para>\(2^\frac49\)</para></problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.2.3.
     </para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(2^\frac49\).
     </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_5c69ff8a-3b0c-4725-841a-c61596f545f4" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Simplify \(k^3\;\cdot\;k^\frac13\).</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(k\)</para><para>\(\frac13k^3\)</para><para>\(3k^\frac13\)</para><para>\(k^\frac{10}3\)</para></problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.2.4.
     </para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(k^\frac{10}3\). \(k^3\cdot k^\frac13=k^{3+\frac13}=k^{\frac93+\frac13}\).
     </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_03d5de9f-8225-48e0-b91a-77c08243ae42" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Simplify \({(m^\frac34)}^{-2}\).</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(m^\frac68\)</para><para>\(\frac1{m^\frac32}\)</para><para>\(m^\frac32\)</para><para>\(-2m^\frac34\)</para></problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.2.4.
     </para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(\frac1{m^\frac32}\). Multiply the exponents, and then get rid of the negative exponent.

     </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="acb98e69-f260-41df-bcc3-6fb665287260" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>True or false:       </para>
          <para>\(\sqrt[4]{-16}\;=\;-2\)</para>

      </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.2.2.
     </para>
          <para>The correct answer is false. You cannot take the even root of a negative number and have a real answer.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="a3499c56-1365-444b-9ce8-3f68f558ef7f" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>True or false:       </para>

          <para>\(\sqrt[3]{-64}\;=\;-4\)</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.2.2.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is true. The cubed root of 64 is 4, and it is possible to have an odd root of a negative number.
     </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="bacf4c60-a706-4321-b22d-e2c39bcc9825" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Which of the following expressions are equivalent to perfect squares?<emphasis>Select the three</emphasis> that apply.</para>
      </problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.2.1.
     </para>
          <para>The correct answer is 25, \(\sqrt{16}\), \(\sqrt[5]{9^5}\).
     </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="bb894585-82dd-45af-8a85-3fd285acc40a" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem><para>Which of the following is equivalent to \({(x^\frac15)}^4\)?</para>
      <para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(x^4\)</para><para>\(x^\frac45\)</para><para>\(x^\frac4{20}\)</para><para>\(x^{20}\)</para></problem>
        <solution>
          
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.2.4.
     </para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(x^\frac45\).
     </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </section>
</content>
</document>
